Commit:     totalwindupflightsystems <totalwindupflightsystems@gmail.com>
CommitDate: Fri Sep 18 18:08:11 2026 -0500

    fix(shim_loop): DF2-H3-SHIM-2 — surface decision_id + pydantic cause on malformed decision payloads
    
    H3ShimLoop.run() funnelled every exception into the bare sentinel "error", so a
    harness author whose decision payload the pydantic models rejected (bad
    llm_call.model, bad wait.reason shape) saw FINAL: error with zero diagnostic —
    the real cause existed only in a shim-side logger the embedder usually has no
    handler attached to.
    
    Additive only; the return contract is unchanged ("error" / "cancelled"):
    
    * LoopError — frozen dataclass carrier holding session_id, decision_id, phase
      (process/result/run), exception_type, first_error_line and the composed
      message. A typed carrier rather than a bare string so a host can branch on
      fields (is_validation -> malformed harness payload vs infrastructure error).
    * on_error=callable — new __init__ kwarg, default None, so every existing
      caller and every existing test keeps working unchanged. Not invoked for
      asyncio.CancelledError; a raising on_error is logged and swallowed so a
      broken diagnostic hook can never break the sentinel contract.
    * last_error — the same carrier on the instance (reset at the start of each
      run) for hosts that register no callback.
    * decision_id context is tracked at the await points. The first /v1/process
      response has no prior decision, so it reports None rather than harvesting or
      fabricating an id from an unparsed payload. ValidationError diagnostics carry
      the "malformed decision payload" prefix plus the first pydantic error line
      rendered as "<loc>: <msg> [type=..., input_value=...]"; every other exception
      gets the "unexpected shim loop error" prefix, so the two classes are tellable
      apart from the message text alone.
    
    Tests (tests/test_shim_loop.py, +8): the headline case drives a REAL H3Client
    whose transport answers a good /v1/process payload and then a malformed
    /v1/result payload, so Decision(**resp.json()) raises the ValidationError in
    production code rather than from an injected mock side effect; it asserts the
    callback fired, the decision_id being answered is named, the exact first
    pydantic error line is present, and run() still returned "error". Also covered:
    the first-process case (decision_id None, no id harvested), default on_error
    None with last_error still populated, CancelledError not invoking the callback,
    validation-vs-generic distinguishability, a raising callback, last_error reset
    between runs, and the frozen carrier.
    
    RED-proved before commit: against the pre-fix source the new tests fail at
    collection (ImportError: cannot import name 'LoopError'); with the carrier
    present but the run() wiring crippled, 6 of the 8 new tests fail.
    
    Gates: .venv/bin/python -m pytest -q -> 369 passed (baseline at HEAD: 361);
    .venv/bin/ruff check . rc=0; .venv/bin/ruff format --check . rc=0. No new
    runtime dependency (stdlib dataclasses + the already-declared pydantic>=2.0).
    
    Co-authored-by: Alexis Okuwa <wojonstech@gmail.com>
